ewpage % ============================================================= % INTRODUCTION (unnumbered, EHR style) % ============================================================= \section*{Introduction} For Estonians, Finland has long served as an `alter ego': the neighbour across the Gulf that shared their language, their Lutheran faith and their nineteenth-century awakening, and that showed, through the television signals picked up in Tallinn from the 1960s, what Estonia might have become had it not spent half a century inside the Soviet Union. ootnote{\autocite[83, 86]{lepppantti2013}.} The comparison is more than nostalgia. It underpins the belief, widespread in the Baltic states, that living standards before the Second World War were comparable to those of the Finns, so that the whole of the gap between the two countries at the restoration of independence in 1991 can be charged to Soviet rule. ootnote{\autocite[154]{norkus_restorations}; \autocite[206]{dellenbrant1992}; \autocite[81--2]{bocharnikova2021}.} In its strongest form, found in the Estonian government's `White Book' on the losses inflicted by occupation, the argument becomes a counterfactual accounting exercise: had Estonia developed as Finland did, its GDP per capita in the early 2000s would have been four to five times what it actually was. ootnote{\autocite[150]{kukk2005}; \autocite[260]{klesment2009}.} Others have doubted that the two economies were ever close, and have pointed out that Finland's own post-war growth was exceptional even by Western European standards, so that the divergence tells us as much about Finland as about Estonia. ootnote{\autocite[18]{norkus2016}; \autocite[201]{koll1994gap}.} What has prevented these claims from being tested is the absence of an annual, internationally comparable GDP series for Estonia before 1995. The existing estimates are partial and rest on conversions that are no longer defensible. Valge's pioneering production-side series covers 1923--38 and was converted into international dollars by assuming, on the authority of Colin Clark's rough figures for 1925--34, that Estonian income per head stood at 90 per cent of Finland's. ootnote{\autocite[2725--6]{valge2003all}; \autocite[60]{clark1938}.} Klesment, Puur and Valge produced a series for 1950--90 from four sectors of material production, scaled to a 1990 level obtained by deflating a purchasing-power-parity estimate for 2005, of undocumented derivation, published by the UN Economic Commission for Europe (UNECE). ootnote{\autocite[39--44]{klesment2010}.} The Maddison Project Database (MPD) fills the gap between 1973 and 1990 by backcasting from modern benchmarks with Soviet net material product (NMP) growth rates, a procedure that Maddison himself regarded as inadmissible for the Soviet Union as a whole, and that places Estonian income in 1989 almost a third above the figure reported here. ootnote{\autocite[307--8]{Maddison1998b}; \autocite{mpd2020}, as quoted in \autocite[155]{norkus_restorations}. See Table~ef{tab:previous}.} The only estimates that are methodologically consistent with the Maddison tradition are the indirect approximations of Norkus and Markevi\v{c}i\={u}t\.{e} for four benchmark years, 1913, 1922, 1929 and 1938. ootnote{\autocite{norkusmark2021}.} Between those years, and for the whole of the Soviet period, there is nothing to which a Finnish, Swedish or Soviet series can be compared. This paper fills that gap. It makes three contributions. First, it constructs a new benchmark estimate of Estonian GDP per capita for 1938, replacing Valge's Finnish bridge with a direct bilateral comparison of consumer prices with Sweden of the kind already used for Lithuania in 1937 and Latvia in 1935, and it uses the new benchmark to recalculate and extend the production-side series to every year from 1919 to 1940. ootnote{; \autocite{norkuslatvia1935}; \autocite{klimantas2023lithuania}; \autocite{Klimantas2024}.} Second, and principally, it

