d into international dollars by assuming, on the authority of Colin Clark's rough figures for 1925--34, that Estonian income per head stood at 90 per cent of Finland's. ootnote{\autocite[2725--6]{valge2003all}; \autocite[60]{clark1938}.} Klesment, Puur and Valge produced a series for 1950--90 from four sectors of material production, scaled to a 1990 level obtained by deflating a purchasing-power-parity estimate for 2005, of undocumented derivation, published by the UN Economic Commission for Europe (UNECE). ootnote{\autocite[39--44]{klesment2010}.} The Maddison Project Database (MPD) fills the gap between 1973 and 1990 by backcasting from modern benchmarks with Soviet net material product (NMP) growth rates, a procedure that Maddison himself regarded as inadmissible for the Soviet Union as a whole, and that places Estonian income in 1989 almost a third above the figure reported here. ootnote{\autocite[307--8]{Maddison1998b}; \autocite{mpd2020}, as quoted in \autocite[155]{norkus_restorations}. See Table~ef{tab:previous}.} The only estimates that are methodologically consistent with the Maddison tradition are the indirect approximations of Norkus and Markevi\v{c}i\={u}t\.{e} for four benchmark years, 1913, 1922, 1929 and 1938. ootnote{\autocite{norkusmark2021}.} Between those years, and for the whole of the Soviet period, there is nothing to which a Finnish, Swedish or Soviet series can be compared. This paper fills that gap. It makes three contributions. First, it constructs a new benchmark estimate of Estonian GDP per capita for 1938, replacing Valge's Finnish bridge with a direct bilateral comparison of consumer prices with Sweden of the kind already used for Lithuania in 1937 and Latvia in 1935, and it uses the new benchmark to recalculate and extend the production-side series to every year from 1919 to 1940. ootnote{\autocite{klimantas2019}; \autocite{norkuslatvia1935}; \autocite{klimantas2023lithuania}; \autocite{Klimantas2024}.} Second, and principally, it provides the first annual series for the Estonian Soviet Socialist Republic (ESSR) covering 1937--95, built from 101 physical product series, agricultural output, transport volumes and employment records drawn from Soviet statistical yearbooks and the Estonian National Archives, and anchored to Bolotin's purchasing-power-parity benchmark for 1990. Third, it splices the two new series to each other, to the 1913 benchmark of Norkus and Markevi\v{c}i\={u}t\.{e}, and to adjusted post-1990 MPD data, to give a continuous series for 1913 and 1919--2022 in both 1990 and 2011 international dollars, together with a consistent population series. Because the interwar and Soviet-period series are built from different sources and anchored to benchmarks fifty-two years apart, their agreement where they overlap, within 4 per cent in each of the years 1937--40 and within 1 per cent at the 1991 splice, is the strongest available check on the whole reconstruction. The new series alters the received picture in three respects. First, Estonia's interwar growth was real but unremarkable: income per head rose by \gr{1919}{1929} per cent a year in the 1920s and \gr{1929}{1938} per cent a year in the 1930s, against \grx{Finland}{1919}{1929} and \grx{Finland}{1929}{1938} per cent in Finland. Estonia emerged from the war of independence in 1919 at \pctfin{1919} per cent of Finnish income per head, close to the near-parity of 1913, but stood at only \pctfin{1938} per cent in 1938. Measured in logarithms, almost half of the gap between the two countries in 1989 had opened before a Soviet soldier crossed the border. Second, the Soviet period divides sharply in two. Between 1950 and 1968 Estonian income per head grew by \gr{1950}{1968} per cent a year, the fastest sustained growth in the country's history before 1995 and faster than in Finland or the Western European core (hereafter `Western core'), though not than the Western periphery (Table~ef{tab:growth}); between 1968 and 1989 it grew by \gr{1968}{1989} per cent a year, and the ground gained was lost again. Estonia stood at \pctwc{1968} per cent of the Western core in 1968 and at \pctwc{1989} per cent in 1989, almost exactly where it had been in 1913. Within the Soviet Union, by contrast, Estonia's position barely changed, from \pctussr{1940} per cent of the Union average in 1940 to \pctussr{1989} per cent in 1989, one of the richest republics throughout. Third, the transition shock was severe: income per head fell by \eeTROUGHpct{} per cent between 1989 and the trough of \eeTROUGHyear{}, and did not regain its 1989 level until 2001. The paper also speaks to a wider literature. The economic history of the Soviet Union has been rewritten over the past two decades from archival sources, but that work has concentrated on Russia; the non-Russian republics, which held half the Union's population, remain almost entirely without modern national accounts. ootnote{; \autocite[277--302]{vonyoinmorys2021}; \autocite{voskoboynikov2021}; \autocite{kalinovsky2022}.} The Estonian series i

