oindent This paper constructs the first internationally comparable annual series of Estonian GDP per capita for 1919--95 and splices it to existing estimates to give an unbroken series for 1913 and 1919--2022 in 1990 and 2011 international dollars, ready for the Maddison Project Database. The reconstruction proceeds in three steps: a new 1938 benchmark based on a bilateral price comparison with Sweden; a recalculated and extended production-side series for 1919--40; and a new series for the Estonian SSR for 1937--95, built from physical output and employment series drawn from Soviet yearbooks and the Estonian archives and anchored to Bolotin's 1990 benchmark. Independently constructed series agree within 4 per cent where they overlap in 1937--40, and within 1 per cent at the 1991 splice. Estonia grew by \gr{1919}{1929} per cent a year in the 1920s and \gr{1929}{1938} per cent in the 1930s, by \gr{1950}{1968} per cent a year in 1950--68 and by \gr{1968}{1989} per cent in 1968--89; the transition cut income per head by a third. Estonia stood at \pctfin{1919} per cent of Finnish income per head in 1919 but at only \pctfin{1938} per cent in 1938: almost half of the gap that separated the two countries in 1989 had opened before Soviet occupation. Finland is a revealing comparator for Estonia, but not a measure of what Estonia lost. \end{abstract} \vspace{0.8cm} {\small	extbf{JEL codes:} N14, O47, E01, P27\par} {\small	extbf{Keywords:} GDP, historical national accounts, Estonia, Finland, Soviet economy, Maddison Project Database\par} \end{titlepage} 
ewpage % ============================================================= % INTRODUCTION (unnumbered, EHR style) % ============================================================= \section*{Introduction} For Estonians, Finland has long served as an `alter ego': the neighbour across the Gulf that shared their language, their Lutheran faith and their nineteenth-century awakening, and that showed, through the television signals picked up in Tallinn from the 1960s, what Estonia might have become had it not spent half a century inside the Soviet Union. ootnote{\autocite[83, 86]{lepppantti2013}.} The comparison is more than nostalgia. It underpins the belief, widespread in the Baltic states, that living standards before the Second World War were comparable to those of the Finns, so that the whole of the gap between the two countries at the restoration of independence in 1991 can be charged to Soviet rule. ootnote{\autocite[154]{norkus_restorations}; \autocite[206]{dellenbrant1992}; \autocite[81--2]{bocharnikova2021}.} In its strongest form, found in the Estonian government's `White Book' on the losses inflicted by occupation, the argument becomes a counterfactual accounting exercise: had Estonia developed as Finland did, its GDP per capita in the early 2000s would have been four to five times what it actually was. ootnote{\autocite[150]{kukk2005}; \autocite[260]{klesment2009}.} Others have doubted that the two economies were ever close, and have pointed out that Finland's own post-war growth was exceptional even by Western European standards, so that the divergence tells us as much about Finland as about Estonia. ootnote{\autocite[18]{norkus2016}; \autocite[201]{koll1994gap}.} What has prevented these claims from being tested is the absence of an annual, internationally comparable GDP series for Estonia before 1995. The existing estimates are partial and rest on conversions that are no longer defensible. Valge's pioneering production-side series covers 1923--38 and was converted into international dollars by assuming, on the authority of Colin Clark's rough figures for 1925--34, that Estonian income per head stood at 90 per cent of Finland's. ootnote{\autocite[2725--6]{valge2003all}; \autocite[60]{clark1938}.} Klesment, Puur and Valge produced a series for 1950--90 from four sectors of material production, scaled to a 1990 level obtained by deflating a purchasing-power-parity estimate for 2005, of undocumented derivation, published by the UN Economic Commission for Europe (UNECE). ootnote{\autocite[39--44]{klesment2010}.} The Maddison Project Database (MPD) fills the gap between 1973 and 1990 by backcasting from modern benchmarks with Soviet net material product (NMP) growth rates, a procedure that Maddison himself regarded as inadmissible for the Soviet Union as a whole, and that places Estonian income in 1989 almost a third above the figure reported here. ootnote{\autocite[307--8]{Maddison1998b}; \autocite{mpd2020}, as quoted in \autocite[155]{norkus_restorations}. See Table~ef{tab:previous}.} The only estimates that are methodologically consistent with the Maddison tradition are the indirect approximations of Norkus and Markevi\v{c}i\={u}t\.{e} for four benchmark years, 1913, 1922, 1929 and 1938. ootnote{\autocite{norkusmark2021}.} Between those years, and for the whole of the Soviet period, there is nothing to which a Finnish, Swedish or Soviet series can be compared. This paper fills that gap. It makes three contributions. First, it constructs a new benchmark estimate of Estonian GDP per capita for 1938, replacing Valge's Finnish bridge with a direct bilateral comparison of consumer prices with Sweden of the kind already used for Lithuania in 1937 and Latvia in 1935, and it uses the new benchmark to recalculate and extend the production-side series to every year from 1919 to 1940. ootnote{\autocite{klimantas2019}; \autocite{norkuslatvia1935}; \autocite{klimantas2023lithuania}; \autocite{Klimantas2024}.} Second, and principally, it provides the first annual series for the Estonian Soviet Socialist Republic (ESSR) covering 1937--95, built from 101 physical product series, agricultural output, transport volumes and employment records drawn from Soviet statistical yearbooks and the Estonian National Archives, and anchored to Bolotin's purchasing-power-parity benchmark for 1990. Third, it splices the two new series to each other, to the 1913 benchmark of Norkus and Markevi\v{c}i\={u}t\.{e}, and to adjusted post-1990 MPD data, to give a continuous series for 1913 and 1919--2022 in both 1990 and 2011 international dollars, together with a consistent population series. Because the interwar and Soviet-period series are built from different sources and anchored to benchmarks fifty-two years apart, their agreement where they overlap, within 4 per cent in each of the years 1937--40 and within 1 per cent at the 1991 splice, is the strongest available check on the whole reconstruction. The new series alters the received picture in three respects. First, Estonia's interwar growth was real but unremarkable: income per head rose by \gr{1919}{1929} per cent a year in the 1920s and \gr{1929}{1938} per cent a year in the 1930s, against \grx{Finland}{1919}{1929} and \grx{Finland}{1929}{1938} per cent in Finland. Estonia emerged from the war of independence in 1919 at \pctfin{1919} per cent of Finnish income per head, close to the near-parity of 1913, but stood at only \pctfin{1938} per cent in 1938. Measured in logarithms, almost half of the gap between the two countries in 1989 had opened before a Soviet soldier crossed the border. Second, the Soviet period divides sharply in two. Between 1950 and 1968 Estonian income per head grew by \gr{1950}{1968} per cent a year, the fastest sustained growth in the country's history before 1995 and faster than in Finland or the Western European core (hereafter `Western core'), though not than the Western periphery (Table~ef{tab:growth}); between 1968 and 1989 it grew by \gr{1968}{1989} per cent a year, and the ground gained was lost again. Estonia stood at \pctwc{1968} per cent of the Western core in 1968 and at \pctwc{1989} per cent in 1989, almost exactly where it had been in 1913. Within the Soviet Union, by contrast, Estonia's position barely changed, from \pctussr{1940} per cent of the Union average in 1940 to \pctussr{1989} per cent in 1989, one of the richest republics throughout. Third, the transition shock was severe: income per head fell by \eeTROUGHpct{} per cent between 1989 and the trough of \eeTROUGHyear{}, and did not regain its 1989 level until 2001. The paper also speaks to a wider literature. The economic history of the Soviet Union has been rewritten over the past two decades from archival sources, but that work has concentrated on Russia; the non-Russian republics, which held half the Union's population, remain almost entirely without modern national accounts. ootnote{\autocite{zhuravskaya2024}; \autocite[277--302]{vonyoinmorys2021}; \autocite{voskoboynikov2021}; \autocite{kalinovsky2022}.} The Estonian series is built with the same methods as the recent series for Lithuania, so that the two can be compared directly and the procedure repeated for the remaining republics, ootnote{\autocite{klimantas2026soviet}.} and it adds a small, open economy with a strong pre-war statistical tradition to the European peripheral economies for which annual interwar accounts now exist. ootnote{; \autocite[1--24]{broadberryinmorys}.} Section~ef{sec:background} sets the scene and reviews the existing estimate

