====================== % I. HISTORICAL BACKGROUND AND PREVIOUS ESTIMATES % ============================================================= \section{Historical background and previous estimates} \label{sec:background} The territory of present-day Estonia passed from Sweden to Russia in 1710--21 and remained within the Russian Empire until 1918. Two inheritances from the Swedish and Baltic-German centuries distinguished it from the rest of the empire. Lutheran parish schooling made the Estonian and Latvian governorates the most literate in the Tsar's dominions, and the early emancipation of the serfs in 1816--19, followed from the 1860s by the sale of farmsteads to Estonian peasants, created a class of proprietors with an orientation towards market agriculture well before 1914. ootnote{\autocite[4--5]{baltichistorykasekamp2017}; \autocite[149--66]{eellend2010}.} By the eve of the First World War Tallinn and Narva had become significant manufacturing centres serving the imperial market, so that Estonia was industrialised out of proportion to its size. ootnote{\autocite[102]{ehbaltics_tarvel}.} The best available estimate places Estonian income per head in 1913 at \ee{1913}~\intlnineteen{}, within a few per cent of Finland's and about half the level of the Western core (Table~ef{tab:relative}). ootnote{\autocite[603]{norkusmark2021}.} The war, the German occupation of 1918 and the war of independence of 1918--20 reversed these gains. Factories were evacuated eastwards, the urban population fell sharply and the Russian market, which had absorbed the bulk of pre-war Baltic manufactures, was lost for good. ootnote{\autocite[107]{ehbaltics_tarvel}; \autocite[193--210]{koll1994gap}.} The new republic was therefore built on the land. The land reform of 1919, among the most radical in Europe, broke up the estates of the Baltic-German nobility and created more than 50,000 new smallholdings; co-operative dairies and export associations reoriented agriculture towards butter, bacon and eggs for the British and German markets, in conscious imitation of Denmark. ootnote{; \autocite{norkus_denmarkisation}.} Agriculture still employed some 63 per cent of the labour force in 1934 (Table~ef

