====================== % I. HISTORICAL BACKGROUND AND PREVIOUS ESTIMATES % ============================================================= \section{Historical background and previous estimates} \label{sec:background} The territory of present-day Estonia passed from Sweden to Russia in 1710--21 and remained within the Russian Empire until 1918. Two inheritances from the Swedish and Baltic-German centuries distinguished it from the rest of the empire. Lutheran parish schooling made the Estonian and Latvian governorates the most literate in the Tsar's dominions, and the early emancipation of the serfs in 1816--19, followed from the 1860s by the sale of farmsteads to Estonian peasants, created a class of proprietors with an orientation towards market agriculture well before 1914. ootnote{\autocite[4--5]{baltichistorykasekamp2017}; \autocite[149--66]{eellend2010}.} By the eve of the First World War Tallinn and Narva had become significant manufacturing centres serving the imperial market, so that Estonia was industrialised out of proportion to its size. ootnote{\autocite[102]{ehbaltics_tarvel}.} The best available estimate places Estonian income per head in 1913 at \ee{1913}~\intlnineteen{}, within a few per cent of Finland's and about half the level of the Western core (Table~ef{tab:relative}). ootnote{\autocite[603]{norkusmark2021}.} The war, the German occupation of 1918 and the war of independence of 1918--20 reversed these gains. Factories were evacuated eastwards, the urban population fell sharply and the Russian market, which had absorbed the bulk of pre-war Baltic manufactures, was lost for good. ootnote{\autocite[107]{ehbaltics_tarvel}; \autocite[193--210]{koll1994gap}.} The new republic was therefore built on the land. The land reform of 1919, among the most radical in Europe, broke up the estates of the Baltic-German nobility and created more than 50,000 new smallholdings; co-operative dairies and export associations reoriented agriculture towards butter, bacon and eggs for the British and German markets, in conscious imitation of Denmark. ootnote{\autocite[64--97]{jorgensen2006}; \autocite{norkus_denmarkisation}.} Agriculture still employed some 63 per cent of the labour force in 1934 (Table~ef{tab:structure}). ootnote{\autocite[181]{morysinterwarinmorys2021}.} The Depression brought a banking crisis in 1931--2 and a devaluation in 1933 that pegged the kroon to sterling, and the authoritarian government installed in 1934 presided over a state-led drive into import-substituting industry now described as `economic nationalism'. ootnote{\autocite[74]{bernankejames01}; \autocite[11--29]{koll1998}; \autocite[118--35]{klesment2005}; \autocite[157--78]{feest2025}.} Output was still expanding when the Molotov--Ribbentrop pact assigned Estonia to the Soviet sphere in August 1939 and Soviet forces occupied the country in June 1940. Soviet rule abolished the interwar economic order within a year. Banks, industry and urban property were nationalised in 1940--1; the German occupation of 1941--4 and the renewed fighting of 1944 followed; and in March 1949 the deportation of some 20,000 people broke the resistance of the countryside to collectivisation, which was essentially complete within months. ootnote{\autocite[265]{taagepera_coll01}; \autocite[12]{deportations_ee}; \autocite[100]{feest2014}.} Industry was rebuilt as a specialised component of the all-Union economy: oil shale and electricity in the north-east, cotton textiles in Narva and Tallinn, and later electrical engineering, with the labour supplied by immigration from Russia on a scale that reduced Estonians to little more than three-fifths of the population by 1989. ootnote{; \autocite[47]{ahderajasalu1992}.} Estonia was consistently among the two or three richest republics of the Union on ev

