 and eggs for the British and German markets, in conscious imitation of Denmark. ootnote{\autocite[64--97]{jorgensen2006}; \autocite{norkus_denmarkisation}.} Agriculture still employed some 63 per cent of the labour force in 1934 (Table~ef{tab:structure}). ootnote{\autocite[181]{morysinterwarinmorys2021}.} The Depression brought a banking crisis in 1931--2 and a devaluation in 1933 that pegged the kroon to sterling, and the authoritarian government installed in 1934 presided over a state-led drive into import-substituting industry now described as `economic nationalism'. ootnote{\autocite[74]{bernankejames01}; \autocite[11--29]{koll1998}; \autocite[118--35]{klesment2005}; \autocite[157--78]{feest2025}.} Output was still expanding when the Molotov--Ribbentrop pact assigned Estonia to the Soviet sphere in August 1939 and Soviet forces occupied the country in June 1940. Soviet rule abolished the interwar economic order within a year. Banks, industry and urban property were nationalised in 1940--1; the German occupation of 1941--4 and the renewed fighting of 1944 followed; and in March 1949 the deportation of some 20,000 people broke the resistance of the countryside to collectivisation, which was essentially complete within months. ootnote{\autocite[265]{taagepera_coll01}; \autocite[12]{deportations_ee}; \autocite[100]{feest2014}.} Industry was rebuilt as a specialised component of the all-Union economy: oil shale and electricity in the north-east, cotton textiles in Narva and Tallinn, and later electrical engineering, with the labour supplied by immigration from Russia on a scale that reduced Estonians to little more than three-fifths of the population by 1989. ootnote{\autocite[158--217]{case_estonia}; \autocite[47]{ahderajasalu1992}.} Estonia was consistently among the two or three richest republics of the Union on every Soviet measure of income and consumption. ootnote{\autocite[169]{schroeder1973}; \autocite[35--6]{bs_livingstandards1988}.} Independence was restored in August 1991; a currency board followed in June 1992, prices were freed and enterprises privatised, and the collapse of eastern markets produced the deepest contraction in the country's recorded history before growth resumed in 1995. ootnote{\autocite[1--8]{wb1993estonia}; \autocite[443--4]{transition_progress}.} The quantitative record of this history is thin. Estonia published no national income accounts before 1940, and the Soviet statistical system measured net material product, which excludes most services and values output at administered prices. Three long series have been attempted, besides the benchmark approximations of Norkus and Markevi\v{c}i\={u}t\.{e}, and their results are compared with the new series in Table~ef{tab:previous}. \input{../../Tables/estonia/T4_previous.tex} The foundation for the interwar period is Valge's production-side estimate of GDP at current and constant prices for 1923--38. ootnote{\autocite{valge2003all}.} Its coverage of agriculture, industry, construction and housing is retained here, but two features limit its use for international comparison. Valge deflated current-price GDP with the cost-of-living index, so that the rapid fall in food prices after 1923 produces a rise of 29 per cent in real income per head in a single year, 1924, when crop, livestock, industrial and transport output each moved by less than 5 per cent. ootnote{\autocite[2718, 2721]{valge2003all}; Klesment's re-deflation with wholesale price indices reduces the jump to 21 per cent (\autocite[29--31]{klesment2008a}; \autocite[37--8]{klesment2010}).} And he expressed the result in international dollars indirectly, by taking Colin Clark's estimate that Estonian output per head averaged 90 per cent of Finland's in 1925--34 and applying that ratio to Maddison's Finnish figures. ootnote{\autocite[2725]{valge2003all}; \autocite[60]{clark1938}.} The resulting level is not far from the one reported here for the 1930s (Table~ef{tab:previous}), but only because a domestic-currency GDP that is too low is offset by a conversion ratio that is too high. Norkus and Markevi\v{c}i\={u}t\.{e} have since produced indirect, income-side approximations for 1913, 1922, 1929 and 1938 that are consistent with the MPD; their 1913 figure is used here as the pre-war anchor, and their 1938 figure, about 5 per cent below the new benchmark, is an independent check on it. ootnote{\autocite{norkusmark2021}; \autocite[154]{norkus_restorations}.} For the Soviet period the only long series is that of Klesment, Puur and Valge, who extrapolated a 1990 level derived from UNECE purchasing-power parities backwards with an official rouble GDP index for 1980--9 and with a composite index of physical output in agriculture, industry, construction and transport for 1950--79. ootnote{\autocite[39--44]{klesment2010}.} The four sectors cover only about two-thirds of GDP, since trade and the non-material sphere are omitted, and the UNECE-based level understates income: even in 1995 the series stands 19 per cent below the estimate obtained here from the MPD's 2011 benchmark. Its growth rate of income per head for 1950--78, 4.4 per cent a year, is well above that of the present series, for reasons discussed in Section~ef{sec:results}. The MPD itself reports Estonian GDP per capita for 1973 and annually from 1980. Its 1990 level descends from Maddison's application of Bolotin's republic-to-Union ratios, taken for 1991 rather than 1990, to a Soviet total itself derived from a bilateral Austria--USSR comparison; its earlier years are extrapolated backwards with NMP growth rates adjusted by the Union-wide ratio of GDP to NMP, a short cut that the literature on material-product accounting had warned against because the ratio varies across countries and over time, and that overstates growth in the final Soviet decades. The MPD figure for 1989 is 32 per cent above the one reported here. ootnote{; \autocite{Campbell1985}; \autocite[279--80]{vonyoinmorys2021}; Table~ef{tab:previous}.} \input{sections/02_method

